        '<li><b>Roads:</b> P. de Soto, A. Pažout, T. Brughmans et al., “Itiner-e: A high-resolution dataset of roads of the Roman Empire”, <i>Scientific Data</i> 12 (2025) 1731, doi:10.1038/s41597-025-06140-z; dataset: Itiner-e static version 2024, Zenodo 17122148 (CC BY 4.0).</li>'
        '<li><b>Provinces, limes, settlements, bases:</b> J. Åhlfeldt, <i>Digital Atlas of the Roman Empire</i> (DARE), Lund – Gothenburg; vector version: github.com/klokantech/roman-empire (CC BY-SA). Place records: imperium.ahlfeldt.se. In the vector version Hadrian’s and the Antonine Wall, the Upper Germanic and Raetian limes, and the Main and Neckar lines lay about 20 km too far north (a projection error: Web Mercator coordinates converted as if ellipsoidal Mercator); I corrected this line by line on 16 November 2026, checked against DARE’s fort points.</li>'
        '<li><b>Coastline and extent of the Empire:</b> Ancient World Mapping Center (University of North Carolina), AWMC geodata, github.com/AWMC/geodata (ODbL); based on the Barrington Atlas of the Greek and Roman World.</li>'
        '<li><b>Rivers and lakes:</b> Natural Earth 1:10 million (public domain); Latin river names from the DARE vector version. Rivers follow their modern beds.</li>'
        '<li><b>Additions to the place records:</b> DARE place records (imperium.ahlfeldt.se, CC BY-SA; 12,501 records retrieved on 12 November 2026); Pleiades: A Gazetteer of Past Places (pleiades.stoa.org, CC BY; data dump of 12 November 2026); Wikidata (CC0) for the Wikipedia articles and Commons images; images from Wikimedia Commons (author and licence under each image; metadata retrieved on 13 November 2026, images downloaded 13–15 November 2026, for 3,790 places in all, scaled to 280 pixels); V. Kaše, P. Heřmánková, A. Sobotková: LIST – Latin Inscriptions in Space and Time v1.2, Zenodo 2024, doi:10.5281/zenodo.10473706 (CC BY 4.0); Coin Hoards of the Roman Empire (CHRE), Ashmolean Museum – Oxford Roman Economy Project (CC BY-NC-SA). The matching and aggregation are my own work.</li>'
        '<li><b>Relief layer:</b> Terrain Tiles (Mapzen / Amazon Web Services Open Data, terrarium, z8; compiled from SRTM, GMTED, ETOPO1 and other open elevation models; downloaded 14 November 2026); reprojected to the map’s projection on a 1.5 km grid and shaded with north-west light and fivefold vertical exaggeration – own processing.</li>'
        '<li><b>Analysis cards:</b> Hanson, J. W. (2016). Cities Database, Version 1.0 (OxREP databases). Accessed 12 November 2026: https://oxrep.web.ox.ac.uk/cities-database. DOI: https://doi.org/10.5287/bodleian:eqapevAn8, and Hanson, J. W. (2016). An Urban Geography of the Roman World, 100 B.C. to A.D. 300. Oxford, Archaeopress; J. Strauss, A. I. Wilson, M. Flohr: OxREP Shipwrecks Database 2.1 (2017; oxrep.site.ox.ac.uk/shipwrecks-database); A. I. Wilson: Database of Roman mines 3.0 (2025; oxrep.web.ox.ac.uk/mines-database, accessed 14 November 2026); all three databases of the Oxford Roman Economy Project (accessed 12 November 2026); CHRE and LIST (see above); Tacitus, Annals 4.5 and Cassius Dio 55.23–24 for the legions; the list of Nicene signatories after the editions of H. Gelzer and C. H. Turner, as compiled by Fourth-Century Christianity (fourthcentury.com). Harvard, Mapping Past Societies (DARMC; CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, accessed 14 November 2026): M. McCormick et al., Geodatabase of Historical Evidence on Roman and Post-Roman Climate (2012-1); J. K. Harper et al., Epidemics 312–749 AD (2017-2); K. Harper, Database of Prices, Wages, and Rents for Roman Egypt (2016-5); J. R. McConnell et al., PNAS 115 (2018) 5726–5731 (published results only, no data series); ORBIS (see the legion march calculator; fastest routes to and from Rome); HGV metadata from the papyri.info repository (github.com/papyri/idp.data, CC BY 3.0; downloaded 14 November 2026), places after Pleiades. The placing on the map and the aggregation are my own work.</li>'
        '<li><b>People in the inscriptions:</b> the “people” field of LIST v1.2, containing the person data of the Epigraphic Database Heidelberg (EDH) (gender, social status, age, origin); own aggregation.</li>'
